# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CompanyOS — Environment Variables
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your actual values.
# NEVER commit .env to Git — it is listed in .gitignore.
# This file (.env.example) IS committed — it shows the required variables
# without exposing real secrets.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MySQL username (default local install is usually root)
DB_USERNAME=root

# MySQL password for the above user
DB_PASSWORD=your_mysql_password_here

# ── Gmail SMTP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full Gmail address used to send verification emails
MAIL_USERNAME=yourcompanyos@gmail.com

# Google App Password — NOT your regular Gmail password.
# Generate one at: Google Account → Security → 2-Step Verification → App Passwords
# It is 16 characters with no spaces, e.g.: abcd efgh ijkl mnop → abcdefghijklmnop
MAIL_PASSWORD=your_16_char_app_password_here

# ── JWT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secret key used to sign and verify JWT tokens.
# Must be at least 32 characters (256 bits).
# Generate a strong value with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# Example (DO NOT use this exact value):
JWT_SECRET=change_this_to_a_long_random_secret_at_least_32_chars

# ── Google OAuth 2.0 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Obtained from Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials
# Instructions:
#   1. Create project at https://console.cloud.google.com
#   2. Configure OAuth Consent Screen (External, add your email as test user)
#   3. Create Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID → Web application
#   4. Authorized JavaScript origins: http://localhost:5173
#   5. Authorized redirect URIs: (none needed for implicit/token flow)
#   6. Copy Client ID and Client Secret below

# BACKEND environment variable — used by GoogleTokenVerifierService to verify tokens
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_google_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# BACKEND environment variable — stored server-side only, NEVER sent to frontend
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_google_client_secret_here

# FRONTEND environment variable (copy to frontend/.env.local)
# VITE_ prefix is required for Vite to expose it to browser code
# The Client ID is public — it is intentionally visible in the browser
# VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_google_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOW TO SET ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES (Windows PowerShell — current session)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# $env:DB_USERNAME        = "root"
# $env:DB_PASSWORD        = "your_mysql_password"
# $env:MAIL_USERNAME      = "yourcompanyos@gmail.com"
# $env:MAIL_PASSWORD      = "your16charapppassword"
# $env:JWT_SECRET         = "your_long_random_secret_here"
# $env:GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID   = "your_google_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com"
# $env:GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET = "your_google_client_secret_here"
#
# Then run the backend from the same PowerShell session:
# cd backend
# .\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOW TO SET ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES (Windows — permanent, via System Settings)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# System Properties → Environment Variables → User variables → New
# Add each variable above with its real value.
# Restart any open PowerShell or IDE sessions after saving.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
